## 3ct GIA Certificate: What to Verify Before Buying

Every 3ct natural diamond purchase should include a GIA certificate review. The grade tells you the color and clarity tier, but the proportion data tells you whether the stone is cut to extract its maximum light. At $48,780, the certificate review takes 10 minutes and is worth every second.

**Table %:** Look for 53–58%. At 53–55%, the stone delivers maximum fire (colored light dispersions). At 56–58%, you get balance between fire and brilliance. Above 60%, fire is suppressed and the stone reads flat.

**Depth %:** Look for 59.5–62.3%. Above 62.5%, a 3ct round starts showing a dark center at 9.4mm face-up — more visible at this size than at 1ct. Below 58.5%, there is risk of light leakage through the base.

**Pavilion angle:** 40.6°–41.0° is the optimal range for maximum brilliance in a round brilliant. Stones at 40.8°–40.9° consistently perform best for light return.

**Fluorescence:** At 3ct, worth noting. Strong Blue fluorescence on a G-color stone can make it appear whiter under UV-rich daylight — sometimes a positive. Strong Yellow fluorescence on a G stone is a negative. None or Faint are neutral. Medium Blue is acceptable at the 3ct tier.

**Girdle:** Thin to Slightly Thick. A Very Thick girdle adds weight without adding face-up diameter. A 3ct stone with a Very Thick girdle hides carat weight in the girdle — you pay for weight you cannot see.

**Culet:** None or Pointed. Any visible culet creates a dark dot visible in the table when viewed face-up. At 9.4mm, this is more noticeable than at smaller sizes.

> **Farzana's Certificate Rule at 3ct:** The most common error I see at the 3ct tier is overpaying for clarity. VS1 over VS2 at 3ct costs $5,860 more with zero visible benefit — you cannot see the difference between VS1 and VS2 at 9.4mm without a loupe. If you have $54,640 to spend (the VS1 price), you are better served spending $48,780 on the VS2 floor stone and directing the $5,860 toward a superior platinum setting. Buy the floor grade, verify the certificate proportions, and invest the surplus where it actually shows.

